WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.120
Film Courage: Can you explain the 8-sequence
template?

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.110
Paul Joseph Gulino, Associate Professor at
Chapman University Dodge College of Film and

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.110
Media Arts: Sure.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:11.019
This originates in two ways, two places.

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:19.620
It originates in Hollywood history by the
ancient of the one reel film (35 millimeter

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.110
film) that became standardized by the first
decade of the 20th century.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:32.980
The first movies by that time (by 1910) they
are single reel experiences that are 10 minutes

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:33.980
long.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:43.339
And for various reasons in the 1910 era Hollywood
split in two directions from the single reel

00:00:43.339 --> 00:00:50.899
film to what we call full length features
(multi reel films - hour and a half to two

00:00:50.899 --> 00:01:00.710
hours) and in the other direction to the serial
which is a reel or two installment series.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:10.400
The reason you have the origin of sequences
is that in the US the distribution system

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.924
was so rigorously one reel a week or two reels
a week that when the start to change that

00:01:14.924 --> 00:01:19.880
when the started to generate full-length feature
films they were still distributed a couple

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.460
of reels a week.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:28.280
So if you look at manuals of the time (screenwriting
manuals) there were about 60 titles that came

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:33.320
in the 1910’s on how to write a photoplay
because the market was wide open and people

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.790
wanted to write these things.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:42.420
You’ll get instructions about making sure
each reel has a climax to it so that the audience

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.380
will be interested in seeing the next reel
and then they will see the next one after

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:52.060
that so it was almost like a limited series
(a feature film).

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:57.450
By the late 1910’s you are seeing the distribution
system is changing and you are seeing in theaters

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:01.790
what we would normal experience as a full-length
feature.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:12.060
But the idea of writing by the reel survived
into the 20’s and 30’s and you can see

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.859
it the way that the continuity scripts are
marked.

00:02:15.859 --> 00:02:18.329
They are often marked by sequence letter.

00:02:18.329 --> 00:02:27.969
Sequence A, B, C, D. And that is where what
we call sequences (the nomenclature) comes

00:02:27.969 --> 00:02:29.719
from.

00:02:29.719 --> 00:02:38.450
What Frank Daniel discovered in teaching in
the eighties he discussed the three-act structure

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:45.650
in terms of developing it and paying it off
but he found that the students struggled with

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:49.709
the middle part of the script because it’s
intimidating.

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:53.129
How do you fill up 60 pages in the middle?

00:02:53.129 --> 00:02:59.870
And usually 30 pages to set it up, 60 pages
in the middle, and 30 pages in the third act.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:01.980
So he revived this idea of sequences.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:08.779
He said well don’t think of it as one act
of 60 pages.

00:03:08.779 --> 00:03:18.510
Think of it as four 15-minute scripts that
each one builds on the last so thus the sequence

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.299
approach is born.

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:24.010
You’re not going to tackle the whole 120
pages.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:29.120
Just figure out the first 15 minutes (the
first one) and then the next 15 minutes.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.669
That’s going to lead to some kind of major
problem for our character.

00:03:33.669 --> 00:03:36.700
So then they are going to try to explore that
for the next 15 minutes.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:42.269
And the effect is actually very liberating
because you end up not worrying about how

00:03:42.269 --> 00:03:46.450
you are going to fill up these pages but about
how you are going to trim down because now

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:47.969
you have all of this material.

00:03:47.969 --> 00:03:54.969
But it also helps you explore a premise really
fully.

00:03:54.969 --> 00:03:59.811
You have a character, let’s just take a
classic dramatic instructions, a character

00:03:59.811 --> 00:04:01.499
wants something and there are obstacles okay?

00:04:01.499 --> 00:04:05.189
At the end of the first act we know what they
want and we know what the obstacles are going

00:04:05.189 --> 00:04:07.379
to be.

00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:09.930
What is the character going to do?

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.760
The character doesn’t know what the movie
is about.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.919
The character just thinks “This is easy,
I can solve this.”

00:04:15.919 --> 00:04:19.810
So they try something and this is in your
conceptualization of the story.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.610
The character tries the easiest thing they
can try.

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:26.380
And then the filmmaker, the screenwriter comes
up with an obstacle of why that doesn’t

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:29.250
work and that’s maybe 10-15 minutes.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.180
Alright now they’ve got to try something
else.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:32.230
What’s that going to be?

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:37.850
And you can develop it and each sequence has
its own integrity ideally that what the paradigm

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:40.919
is that each sequence is going to have three
acts also.

00:04:40.919 --> 00:04:42.520
It’s going to have some kind of set-up.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.680
You don’t have to have as much set-up because
you know the characters but you are going

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.060
to have to introduce new circumstances.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:52.280
They are going to try to get something and
then it’s going to end with some kind of

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:56.590
resolution usually negative because if it’s
positive then the movie is over.

00:04:56.590 --> 00:05:02.430
But it leads to the next sequence and then
the next one.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:10.090
So you wind up with actually kind of a nested
structure because the dramatic scenes have

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:11.090
the same three acts.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:13.080
That’s why we tend to like three acts.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:15.370
It depends on how you define it.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:21.460
But if you understand it as working with tension
were tension is just putting something in

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.780
the audiences mind (hope and fear).

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:28.120
Your character wants something, are they going
to get it or not?

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:29.120
That’s what I’m wondering about.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.740
And if I care about the character then I’m
going to stay tuned because I’m going to

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.159
want to see the answer to that.

00:05:33.159 --> 00:05:37.490
So you don’t need more than three acts for
that, you just need to set that up and then

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:39.820
you need to develop it and then you get the
answer.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:44.050
So each act set up the whole thing.

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:49.539
Then each sequence has its own…okay the
character wants something and then there are

00:05:49.539 --> 00:05:50.539
problems.

00:05:50.539 --> 00:05:54.699
And then each scene has something the character
wants something and then there are obstacles.

00:05:54.699 --> 00:06:02.710
So it’s like this iterative structure that
keeps the audience involved because we’re

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:08.750
constantly wondering about how that’s going
to come out for the character.

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:12.710
And one thing that we cover in the book The
Science of Screenwriting: The Neuroscience

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:17.560
Behind Storytelling Strategies is this process
of connection to a main character and what

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:21.720
the theories are about why we have that.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:29.509
It’s certainly important if you come into
a park and you come up on a tennis game and

00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:33.699
two people are really battling each other
it may be interesting but it’s not going

00:06:33.699 --> 00:06:36.610
to be dramatic to you because you don’t
know them.

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:41.800
But if you do know one of them, if you love
one them and you know that they just mortgaged

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:47.879
your house and put everything on this game
and then you know the other person is a hustler

00:06:47.879 --> 00:06:49.419
then every…Oh my gosh!

00:06:49.419 --> 00:06:51.320
You’ve got something that is transformational.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:58.419
But you’ve got to make that connection emotionally.

00:06:58.419 --> 00:07:08.030
To get back about the sequence structure you
have many versions of the three acts in the

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:09.819
sequences and then in the scenes.

00:07:09.819 --> 00:07:14.139
And that is the suspense (the tension) that
keeps us interested.

00:07:14.139 --> 00:07:17.780
We also have a chapter on contrast in the
film.

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:27.280
And that plays also into this approach which
is in order to maintain audience attention

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:36.569
the stimulus has to be changed frequently
otherwise you zone out, you lose connection,

00:07:36.569 --> 00:07:41.780
you will think about other things and you
need reset the audiences brain periodically.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:46.750
And filmmakers do it you can see evidence
of it in changing light and dark, loud and

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:48.189
soft, fast and slow.

00:07:48.189 --> 00:07:51.080
But you also see it in tension and release.

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.400
Tension maintained for too long gets tedious.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.960
But if you release it and give it a chance
to reset then you can go on to build it up

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:04.419
again and again and again until you reach
the culmination of the picture and then finally

00:08:04.419 --> 00:08:09.680
you try to release it entirely and get people
to say “Yes, I want to see that movie again.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:11.379
I’m going to tell all my friends I’m going
to see it.”

00:08:11.379 --> 00:08:14.840
Film Courage: Is it based on a time limit?

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:21.470
Is there an actual science down to the minute
of what we can handle in terms of when our

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:22.590
mind wanders?

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:25.830
Professor Gulino: My understanding is (and
this is something that Dr. Connie Shears might

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:30.410
be able to confirm) but our attention can
maybe last about 3 to 5 minutes.

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:33.820
And then we need to reset it somehow.

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:39.510
And dramatic scenes tend to be about 3 minutes,
usually about three pages.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:45.300
And before you need to have some new character
enter, you cut away, you have a new development,

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:50.079
something to scramble it up.

00:08:50.079 --> 00:08:54.019
The basic premise of this book, the inspiration
of doing the The Science of Screenwriting:

00:08:54.019 --> 00:08:59.690
The Neuroscience Behind Storytelling Strategies
was if you see patterns in movies and you

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:04.240
see patterns in the advice about movies is
there a deeper reason for it?

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:07.660
Is there a physiological basis for it.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:13.790
There is a New Yorker [Magazine] cartoon that
kind of illustrates that idea.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:20.110
There’s a movie theater and it’s filled
with cats.

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:22.950
All of the cats are the whole audience.

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:27.709
And on screen is a shoe with a shoelace that
is loose.

00:09:27.709 --> 00:09:34.480
Okay, a cat is going to find that completely
enthralling, a human being maybe not so much.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:38.529
But what we do find enthralling, you’re
going to see the patterns.

00:09:38.529 --> 00:09:43.980
We are going to look for certain things and
be satisfied by certain things.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:51.029
The physiology kind of determines what we’re
seeing on screen.

00:09:51.029 --> 00:09:56.350
I kind of see the book (as I mentioned) as
an argument settler.

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:58.110
What’s fashionable.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:09:59.720
What advice is essential, what isn’t.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:01.110
What do we need to know?

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:03.410
What do we not need to know?

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:09.550
Go back to the scientific basis for things
and see is there something there that dictates

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:12.560
it or is it just an option?

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:14.870
That’s part of the inspiration for it.

